TI  - Audit of neonatal sepsis and compliance with the antibiotic guidelines in a tertiary neonatal unit
JO  - Journal of Paediatrics and Child Health
PY  - 2014
AB  - Background: Sepsis is common in very preterm (<32 weeks) and very low birth weight (<1500 g) infants, contributing to significant mortality and morbidity. It is therefore important to monitor the rate of sepsis and pathogens isolated to provide informative clinical practice guidelines and antibiotic regimes. This audit aimed to describe early and late onset sepsis, including clinicians' compliance with antibiotic guidelines in a tertiary neonatal unit. Method: Infants born less than 32 weeks' gestation or less than 1500 grams admitted to the Newborn Intensive and Special Care at the Royal Women's Hospital from 1 July-31 December 2011 were included. Strict definitions of sepsis and compliance with antibiotic guidelines were applied. Data on late onset sepsis (LOS) was also compared with a historical cohort.1 Results: One hundred and seventy-two infants met the inclusion criteria, with 152 having blood culture evaluations for early onset sepsis (EOS) and 58 having 109 evaluations for LOS. Definite EOS occurred in 1.3%, with Escherichia coli the causative organism in both cases. The rate of definite LOS was 22%, with coagulase negative staphylococcus the leading pathogen. Non-compliance with antibiotic continuation beyond 72 hours in infants with negative blood cultures was evident (6% in the EOS group and 17% in the LOS group). Conclusions: The rate of EOS is comparable to previous reports and the rate of LOS has remained stable over a three year period. Reduction in duration of antibiotics is required to improve compliance with antibiotic guidelines.
