TI  - VANCOMYCIN TOXICITY IN PRETERM NEONATES ON THE NEONATAL INTENSIVE CARE UNIT
JO  - Archives of Disease in Childhood
PY  - 2022
AB  - Aims Acute kidney injury (AKI) is an under-acknowledged but increasing concern in extremely preterm babies in neonatal intensive care units (NICU). It is multifactorial in aetiology but can be both caused and identified by elevated levels of nephrotoxic, renally-excreted drugs. Vancomycin is the empirical antibiotic choice for late onset sepsis, and on our NICU it is normally administered by continuous infusion, with dose determined by creatinine levels, and some occasional use of an intermittent dosing regime. We aimed to identify the risk of high vancomycin levels and associated AKI in preterm babies during the NICU stay. Methods We retrospectively identified all preterm babies of gestation <28 weeks on NICU in two 9-month epochs representing two separate continuous infusion dosing schedules, with a 1-month watershed period between epochs: Epoch 1 (December 2019 to August 2020) and Epoch 2 (October 2020 to June 2021). Babies treated with vancomycin were identified by export of vancomycin monitoring data from the electronic medical records and further data was extracted from the hospital electronic medical records. For each epoch we identified babies that had normal (VancN), or at least one episode of toxic (VancT) levels, and those that did not receive vancomycin (Vanc0). An episode of vancomycin toxicity was defined as levels >20.0mg/L (pre-dose assay) or >25.0mg/L (continuous infusion). AKI was defined as >50% rise in creatinine during an episode of vancomycin toxicity. We explored risk factors including gestational age (GA), birth weight, the presence of a patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) and the total duration of vancomycin exposure. To quantify the AKI, we examined the maximum vancomycin concentration and the% change in creatinine pre and post VancT. This was a service evaluation and so did not require ethical approval. Results 118 infants admitted during these periods were identified: Epoch 1, n=64 and Epoch 2, n=54. Table 1 shows the number of babies in each Epoch with toxic levels. In Epoch 1, 34/64 (54%) received vancomycin and 25/34 (39%) had toxic levels. In Epoch 2, 31/54 (57%) received vancomycin and 24/31 (44%) had toxic levels. In both Epochs, VancT was associated with longer duration of vancomycin exposure but only in Epoch 1 with lower GA, birth weight and presence of a PDA. The median maximum vancomycin level per episode was 28.4mg/L (range: 21.0 to 56.3) in Epoch 1 and 29.3 mg/ L (range: 20.4 to 41.2) in Epoch 2. The median% change in creatinine per episode was +16.7% (range: -8.7 to +213.7) in Epoch 1 and +8.6% (range: -17.5 to +386.5) in Epoch 2 (figure 1). A >50% rise in creatinine occurred in 12/43 (28%) episodes in Epoch 1 and 6/35 (17%) episodes in Epoch 2. Conclusion There is a high incidence of toxic vancomycin levels in preterm babies on NICU. Risk is predicted by duration of vancomycin exposure and potentially birthweight, GA and PDA presence. Our dosing changes did not reduce the number of babies with vancomycin toxicity but may have reduced the impact on renal function.. (Figure Presented).
DO  - 10.1136/archdischild-2022-rcpch.282
