TI  - 131 Early antibiotic use and retinopathy of prematurity: a single-center retrospective study
JO  - Journal of AAPOS
PY  - 2025
AB  - Introduction: Retinopathy of prematurity (ROP) has been linked to neonatal sepsis, with antibiotic use suggested as a connection. However, investigations on this association are limited. Given antibiotics’ role in gut dysbiosis and the gut-retina axis, we assessed whether exposure to different antibiotic classes is associated with incidence of treatment-necessary ROP. Methods: Retrospective analysis was performed on preterm screened for ROP between January 2016 and May 2023. The primary outcome measure was severe ROP requiring treatment. Associations were evaluated between treated ROP and systemic antibiotic exposure within the first 2 and 6 months of life. Multivariate adjustment included birth weight (BW), gestational age (GA), bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD), intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH), necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC), and sepsis. Results: A total of 720 patients were included, with 50 treated for ROP. Patients with type 1 ROP showed lower BWs (P < 0.0001), younger GAs (P < 0.0001), and higher rates of BPD (P < 0.001), IVH (P < 0.001), and bacterial infection (P < 0.01). No association was noted with NEC. After multivariable adjustment, odds of treatment were 3.2x higher for infants receiving cephalosporins/carbapenems/monobactams (95% CI, 1.14-8.95). When restricted to BW < 750 grams or GA < 27 weeks, rates of other comorbidities were not significantly different, yet this association remained significant (OR = 3.2; 95% CI, 1.14-8.93). Antifungals trended toward significance on multivariate analysis (OR = 2.74; 95% CI, 0.90-8.4). Conclusion/Relevance: Early exposure to specific antibiotic classes, particularly cephalosporins, carbapenems, and monobactams, may be associated with treatment-necessary ROP. Given the broad-spectrum coverage and significance after adjusting for sepsis and NEC, this association may be driven by gut dysbiosis.
