TI  - 78 A case of septic arthritis and spondylitis caused by Brucella melitensis – The subtlety of the Pedro Pons sign and the use of an alternative formulation of doxycycline
JO  - Clinical Infection in Practice
PY  - 2025
AB  - Introduction: Brucella is one of the most common zoonoses worldwide. It is a gram negative coccobacillus that invades via mucosa, activating neutrophils and macrophages leading to granulomas, abscesses and necrosis. It presents with non-specific features and can affect multiple body systems; arthritis, endocarditis, meningoencephalitis, pneumonitis. It requires a prolonged course of multiple antibiotics and carries mortality rate of 1–5 % and relapse rate of up to 10 %. Case: A 70 year old woman presented with three months of feeling intermittently unwell with fevers and malaise, along with three days of a painful left knee. She presented three weeks after returning from Cairo and symptoms had started 2 days after arriving in Egypt. During the trip she had consumed milk and cheese from local shops. Her symptoms had not improved with oral clarithromycin from her GP. Examination revealed a hot, swollen, tender left knee. Blood cultures and joint aspirate both grew gram negative coccobacilli, identified 5 days later as Brucella melitensis. MRI left knee revealed joint effusion and arthritic changes. MRI of the thoracic spine 13/12/23 showed a small focus of oedema at the end plate of T5-6, which was felt to be non-specific. However, back pain persisted and a second opinion was sought. This identified the previous findings as the Pedro-Pons sign and hence confirmed a diagnosis of Brucellar spondylitis. Oral doxycycline, oral rifampicin and intravenous gentamicin was commenced for 1 week. The patient improved clinically and was discharged with the two oral antibiotics to complete a total of 12 weeks. She represented 10 days later due to nausea and dizziness. The nausea was felt to be related to Doxycycline and so the formulation was changed from doxycycline hyclate to doxycycline monohydrate with excellent resolution. She was also managed for postural hypotension. Discussion: Anterior superior end plate erosion, the Pedro Pons sign, is a subtle yet pathognomonic imaging finding in Brucellar spondylitis. Identifying bone involvement in patients with Brucellosis is crucial as it means that duration of antibiotics should be extended from 6 to 12 weeks. Switching the formulation of doxycycline in this case allowed the first line antibiotics to be continued. Whilst doxycycline monohydrate is more expensive, its lower rate of gastrointestinal side effects may aid in preventing premature cessation. Moreover, the second line treatment includes ciprofloxacin, less preferable given the concerns about fluoroquinolones raised by the MHRA and the antimicrobial stewardship implications highlighted by the WHO AWaRe classification.
