AB  - Background: Blood culture (BC) was introduced at the National Hospital in Timor-Leste in 2018, however uptake was low in the neonatal unit (NNU). Sepsis caused 401/1809 (22%) of admissions and 24/161 deaths in 2022, but only 100 BCs were collected. Sepsis was diagnosed clinically and babies treated with empirical antibiotics for 5 days if at risk of sepsis, or 7+ days for suspected sepsis. The Neosepsis project (March 2023-ongoing) aims to improve BC and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) collection, rational antibiotic use and clinical outcomes through quality improvement (QI) methods. Methods: Protocols and training have been introduced for (i) collection of BC; (ii) reduction of BC contaminants and improvement of BC volume; and (iii) CSF collection using plan-do-study-act (PDSA) cycles. Demographic, prescription, outcome and laboratory data is collected on all babies commenced on antibiotics in the NNU. Results: Pre-post PDSA cycle results from 2023 to 2024 include: (i) BC collection increase from 8/month to 118/month, (ii) BC contamination reduction from 8% to < 5%, (iii) CSF collection from 0 to 25. Conclusions: Established QI methodology can change practice in NNUs in resource-limited settings. At our site BC is now routine practice, with acceptable contamination rates. Utilising lessons from these PDSA cycles further improvements are expected, including antimicrobial stewardship and clinical outcomes.
